{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 0, "model_name": "google/gemma-3n-e4b-it", "content": "Raymond III Count of Tripoli ruled the County of Tripoli from 1154 until his death in 1199. He was a significant figure in the Crusades a powerful and complex ruler navigating the treacherous political landscape of the Levant. His reign was marked by military campaigns diplomatic maneuvering and economic development.\n\n*Early Life and Accession*\n\nRaymond was born around 1120 the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Ermengarde of Hauteville. He received a solid education and was groomed for leadership. He inherited the County of Tripoli upon his father's death in 1154. Immediately he faced challenges including threats from Muslim rulers and internal political factions. Raymond solidified his position through skillful diplomacy and military strength. He inherited a county that was economically prosperous and strategically important a vital link between Europe and the East. His early years focused on consolidating power and defending Tripoli's borders.\n\nRaymond III’s reign was characterized by constant warfare. He fought numerous battles against the Ayyubid dynasty under Saladin and his successors. He engaged in alliances with other Crusader states like Antioch and Jerusalem to counter Ayyubid expansion. These campaigns were often costly and resulted in territorial losses but Raymond consistently defended Tripoli's independence. He also faced challenges from within his own county dealing with rebellious vassals and power-hungry nobles. He successfully maintained control through a combination of force and negotiation.\n\n*Diplomacy and Alliances*\n\nRaymond was a skilled diplomat adept at forging alliances and maintaining fragile peace. He actively engaged in negotiations with Muslim rulers seeking to secure trade routes and prevent conflict. He understood the importance of economic ties and fostered relationships with merchants from various regions. He formed alliances with the Knights Templar and other military orders bolstering Tripoli’s defenses. His diplomatic efforts were crucial in maintaining a degree of stability in the region despite the ongoing warfare. He sought to balance the needs of Tripoli with the broader interests of the Crusader states.\n\n*Economic and Cultural Development*\n\nRaymond III oversaw a period of economic and cultural flourishing in Tripoli. The county remained a major trading center benefiting from its strategic location on the Mediterranean coast. He encouraged the development of industries such as textiles and shipbuilding. Tripoli's cultural life thrived attracting scholars and artists from different backgrounds. He supported the construction of churches mosques and other public buildings. His reign witnessed a blend of Christian and Islamic cultures reflecting the cosmopolitan nature of the region. He invested in infrastructure improving roads and fortifications. His administration was known for its relative efficiency and fairness.\n\nRaymond III died in 1199 leaving behind a legacy of resilience and leadership. He was remembered as a strong defender of the Crusader states a shrewd diplomat and a capable ruler. His reign shaped the history of Tripoli and the Levant during a turbulent period. His efforts to maintain stability and prosperity in the face of constant threats earned him respect from both Christian and Muslim leaders. He is considered one of the most important Counts of Tripoli.\n", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -0.0017849955474957824}, {"token": "▁III", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Count", "logprob": -2.861018856492592e-06}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "▁ruled", "logprob": -0.2533552050590515}, {"token": "▁the", "logprob": -0.2717786729335785}, {"token": "▁County", "logprob": -0.06198554113507271}, {"token": "▁of", "logprob": -3.576278118089249e-07}, {"token": "▁Tripoli", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token